    <div class="post-container" id="fpc-2683916">
                      
		<div class="post" id="post-2683916">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2683916)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2683916">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="1324229"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1478222870 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">04 Nov 2016 01:27</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2683916">
									

<p>I don't have an issue with this, honestly. While I certainly like having characters grow and change over the course of the narrative, I think that entries moving the plot forward or providing additional detail are fine, provided that they are competently done and provide enough interesting material to justify their existence. I feel that this is certainly one of those cases. Indeed, I almost kind of like the relative lack of characterization for the O5's. In this context, the personal quirks and hang-ups of O5-2 are irrelevant, as are the hopes and dreams of O5-7, outside of &quot;making sure we don't all die horribly.&quot; In-universe, they only matter in the context of doing their jobs, which justifies the lack of focus on personal drama in favor of focusing on the fact that Space Loki is wrecking our collective shit.</p>
<p>Or it could simply be a missed opportunity to give some characterization. Either way, voted up for trippy technicolor murder Turk.</p>
<p>Also, the &quot;film of the collective horrible things that humanity has done&quot; thing reminded me a bit of the &quot;Black Tape&quot; arc from Yu-Yu Hakusho. Not a complaint, just sayin'.</p>

							</div>

								
					
